llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
126): the hand-off between a device split and a host split runs on the
device stream instead of in blocking copies, so the host waits once per
hand-off instead of once per tensor. Not gfx803-specific code; measured on
gfx803.

WHY
---
With a MoE model's routed experts in system RAM, every layer of a
generated token hands work from the GPU to the CPU and back: the CPU split
reads the FFN input and the expert ids from the GPU, and the next GPU split
reads the CPU's expert outputs. The scheduler copied each such tensor with
a blocking copy: the CPU split waited for the head event (patch 112), then
synchronized a separate device-to-host copy per input, and the GPU split
after it waited for the whole device stream before a blocking
host-to-device copy, and only then launched its kernels. On an RX 590 that
is tens of microseconds per layer on the critical path, 40 layers per
token, while both sides wait for each other.

WHAT
----
- Right after the head of a device split that is followed by a host split,
  the host split's inputs that this device computed are queued as
  asynchronous device-to-host copies on the device stream, before the
  head's event is recorded. The host split then waits for that one event
  and copies nothing itself. The CPU backend's compute buffer is the
  device's pinned host buffer (llama_context picks it), so the copies are
  truly asynchronous.
- A device split's input computed by the CPU is queued as an asynchronous
  host-to-device copy on the device stream, without waiting for the
  device first: the stream orders the copy after the device's earlier use
  of the destination and before the kernels that read it, and the CPU
  wrote the source synchronously. An event is recorded after the copy, and
  any later host split waits for it before it computes, because the
  allocator may hand the copy's source memory to that split's tensors.

Nothing is computed differently. GGML_SCHED_ASYNC_HOST_COPY=0 restores the
blocking copies.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 590, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B heretic Q4_K_M):
- llama-perplexity, 2 chunks of 512 at -ub 1 (every token a one-token
  decode), routed experts in system RAM: logits byte-identical to the
  blocking copies, with and without hot experts (patch 140).
- llama-server, ten chat prompts not used for anything else, 400 greedy
  tokens each, generation tok/s (mean): with llama.cpp's fit placing whole
  layers of experts in VRAM 31.5 -> 33.2; all experts in RAM (-ncmoe 99)
  28.8 -> 30.9; with hot experts and HIP graphs off 36.6 -> 39.7. Per
  token, the CPU's wait for its inputs fell from 9.0 to 7.5 ms and the
  GPU's wait for the CPU's output from 3.4 to 2.7 ms.
